Outdegree multiset is invariant under node renaming.

A: {7,5,5,4,3,3,2,2,2,1,1,1}
B: {7,5,5,4,3,3,2,2,2,2,1,0}

They differ: A has no outdegree-0 node and three outdegree-1 nodes; B has one 0, one 1, and four 2s.

So no degree-preserving bijection exists.

ANSWER: NO

Compare out-degree multisets, which must be preserved by node renaming.

A: {1×4, 2×1, 3×2, 4×2, 5×2, 6×1}
B: {0×1, 1×2, 2×2, 3×2, 4×2, 5×2, 6×1}

These differ: B has a 0-outdegree node and a different count of 1s/2s. So no renaming can match them.

ANSWER: NO

Degree multisets must match under any node renaming.

A: {6,6,5,3,3,3,3,2,2,1,1,1}
B: {6,6,5,3,3,3,3,2,2,2,1,0}

A has no degree-0 node and three degree-1 nodes; B has one degree-0 node and only one degree-1 node. This cannot be fixed by renaming.

ANSWER: NO

Compare out-degree multisets.

A: {1,1,1,2,2,3,3,4,5,6,7}

B: {0,1,1,2,2,2,3,3,4,5,6,7}

They differ: A has three degree-1 nodes and two degree-2 nodes; B has two degree-1 nodes and three degree-2 nodes, plus a degree-0 node.

Isomorphism must preserve degree multisets.

ANSWER: NO

Out-degrees must match as a multiset under any node renaming.

A: {6,5,5,4,4,3,2,2,2,1,1,1}
B: {6,5,5,4,4,3,2,2,2,2,1,0}

These differ: A has three 1s and no 0; B has one 1, one 0, and four 2s.

ANSWER: NO
